{
  "id": "find-k-closest-elements",
  "questionId": "find-k-closest-elements",
  "slug": "find-k-closest-elements",
  "title": "Find K Closest Elements",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "the answer is always a single contiguous window of k elements in the sorted array - once you know where that window starts, you're done",
    "there are only n - k + 1 possible starting positions for that window - can you binary search over those positions instead of sorting by distance?",
    "for a candidate window start i, compare x - arr[i] (how far the left edge is) to arr[i+k] - x (how far the element just past the right edge is) - whichever is smaller tells you which side to keep",
    "binary search left from 0 to (n - k): if x - arr[mid] > arr[mid+k] - x, the window should move right (mid+1 becomes the new left bound), otherwise shrink the right bound to mid; when left meets right, that's the window start"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "breaking distance ties in favor of the larger value instead of the smaller one",
    "treating the answer as k independently chosen elements instead of one contiguous window in the sorted array",
    "off-by-one on the binary search bounds for the window start, e.g. allowing a start index that would run the window past the end of the array"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array of integers sorted in ascending order, an integer\n`k`, and an integer `x`.\n\n**Return the `k` elements of the array that are closest to `x`, sorted in ascending order.**\n\nIf two elements are equally close to `x`, prefer the smaller of the two.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "arr is sorted in ascending order",
      "1 <= k <= length of arr",
      "ties in distance are broken in favor of the smaller value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "x smaller than every element, or larger than every element",
      "k equal to the full length of the array",
      "a tie in distance right at the boundary of the window"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 4, x = 3",
        "output": "[1, 2, 3, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 2, 3, 4]",
        "explanation": "dropping 5 (distance 2) keeps the 4 closest values to 3"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 4, x = -1",
        "output": "[1, 2, 3, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 2, 3, 4]",
        "explanation": "everything is to the right of x, so the 4 smallest values are the closest"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 2, x = 3",
        "output": "[2, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[2, 3]",
        "explanation": "2 and 4 are tied at distance 1 from 3, so the smaller one (2) wins the tie, alongside 3 itself at distance 0"
